{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-109390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-109390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55dcf6af56f0ddb0867fc95ebd680994bdbc633a4a927f232420da79f8436cf8"} {"question" "q-109390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-109390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-109390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55dcf6af56f0ddb0867fc95ebd680994bdbc633a4a927f232420da79f8436cf8"} {"question" "q-110381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question