}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55dcf6af56f0ddb0867fc95ebd680994bdbc633a4a927f232420da79f8436cf8"} {"question" "q-110377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -9 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55dcf6af56f0ddb0867fc95ebd680994bdbc633a4a927f232420da79f8436cf8"} {"question" "q-110375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(4)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =2:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nComputing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-2I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55dcf6af56f0ddb0867fc95ebd680994bdbc633a4a927f232420da79f8436cf8"} {"question" "q-110457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 