ate.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =-5$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(-4)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-4.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-5:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(-5).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(-5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-5).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nComputing $A+5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+5I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(-4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-5)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/state.json" "file_sha256" "7db9f7a1f0f6d875355678512efd694d8eb60e998daa5d0b4734251b5fbd5d4c"} {"question" "q-110397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55dcf6af56f0ddb0867fc95ebd680994bdbc633a4a927f232420da79f8436cf8"}